  "modern": "252. The oxygenated liquor prepared in this way is very effectual in bleaching the fluffs which are immersed in it, but it at the same time possesses an odour so suffocating as to render its use unpleasant and even injurious to the workmen employed in the process, should any of it escape. I have witnessed, (says O'Reilly) in an extensive manufactory near Paris, the cruel sufferings experienced by the wretched workmen from these suffocating vapours; I have seen them rolling on the ground in the excess of agony. Frequently even fever disorders are the consequence of the first effects produced by the oxygenated liquor. Dr. Charmen gives a long account of the fittings which he underwent in his course of experiments with this acid. It produces symptoms completely resembling those of a violent cold, but which go off in a day or two after having ceased from the use of the liquor."
